WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.581
What k1 and b do, for a mathematician.

2
00:00:02.861 --> 00:00:06.723
Fix a term and look at its contribution as a function of tf.

3
00:00:07.003 --> 00:00:12.165
Write K for k1 times (1 minus b plus b times dl / avgdl).

4
00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:24.669
The contribution is proportional to tf / (tf + K), a saturating hyperbola: zero at tf = 0, increasing and concave, with horizontal asymptote 1.

5
00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:35.552
Multiplying by (k1 + 1) scales it so the asymptote is k1 + 1 and, for an average-length document, tf = 1 gives exactly 1.

6
00:00:35.832 --> 00:00:37.731
k1 controls saturation.

7
00:00:38.011 --> 00:00:43.600
With k1 = 0 the function is a step: present or absent, a binary model.

8
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:51.709
As k1 grows, saturation comes later and the function approaches linear tf, which is TF-IDF’s behavior.

9
00:00:51.989 --> 00:00:59.264
Typical values sit between those extremes, so the second mention of a term adds a lot and the twentieth adds almost nothing.

10
00:00:59.544 --> 00:01:01.699
b controls length normalization.

11
00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:09.637
The factor (1 minus b plus b times dl / avgdl) is a convex combination of 1 and the relative length.

12
00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:12.328
At b = 0 length is ignored.

13
00:01:12.608 --> 00:01:23.360
At b = 1 term frequency is judged fully relative to length: a document twice the average length needs twice the occurrences to earn the same contribution.

14
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:39.725
Robertson and Zaragoza frame this as two hypotheses about long documents, verbosity (the author used more words for the same content, so normalize) and scope (the document covers more ground, so don’t), and b as a soft mix of the two.

15
00:01:40.005 --> 00:01:44.464
On values: Lucene and Elasticsearch default to k1 = 1.

16
00:01:44.744 --> 00:01:46.301
2 and b = 0.

17
00:01:46.581 --> 00:01:47.605
75.

18
00:01:47.885 --> 00:01:52.408
The survey reports that experiments generally found values in roughly 0.

19
00:01:52.688 --> 00:01:53.797
5 to 0.

20
00:01:54.077 --> 00:01:55.379
8 for b and 1.

21
00:01:55.659 --> 00:02:06.347
2 to 2 for k1 to work well in many circumstances, while noting that the model gives no guidance on setting them and that the best values depend on the documents and queries.

22
00:02:06.627 --> 00:02:15.160
That last point is your cue: these are hyperparameters you can tune on a labeled query set, which is exactly what lesson 9 teaches you to build.
